# CTYPE(3)

## NAME

isalpha, isupper, islower, isdigit, isxdigit, isalnum, isspace, isblank, ispunct, isprint,
isgraph, iscntrl, isascii, toupper, tolower - character classification and case conversion

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <ctype.h>

int isalpha(int c);

. . .

int toupper(int c);

int tolower(int c);
```

## DESCRIPTION

These macros classify ASCII-coded integer values by table lookup. Each is a predicate returning
nonzero for true, zero for false. *Isascii* is defined on all integer values; the rest are
defined only where *isascii* is true and on the single non-ASCII value EOF (see stdio(3)).

*isalpha*
: *c* is a letter

*isupper*
: *c* is an upper case letter

*islower*
: *c* is a lower case letter

*isdigit*
: *c* is a digit

*isxdigit*
: *c* is a hexadecimal digit

*isalnum*
: *c* is an alphanumeric character

*isspace*
: *c* is a space, tab, carriage return, newline, vertical tab or formfeed

*isblank*
: *c* is a space or a tab. **Note:** C11; v7 had no such test.

*ispunct*
: *c* is a punctuation character (neither control nor alphanumeric)

*isprint*
: *c* is a printing character, code 040(8) (space) through 0176 (tilde)

*isgraph*
: *c* is a printing character other than the space

*iscntrl*
: *c* is a delete character (0177) or ordinary control character (less than 040).

*isascii*
: *c* is an ASCII character, code less than 0200

*Toupper* and *tolower* convert a letter to the other case and return **any other argument
unchanged.**

## SEE ALSO

ascii(7), string(3)

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/ctype_.c, toupper.c, tolower.c, isblank.c, include/ctype.h

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The table is v7's, unchanged, because this terminal is ASCII

Not KOI7. The console driver passes bytes untranslated in both directions, so there was nothing
in the classification to re-derive.

### isprint(' ') is true now, and it was not in v7

v7 left bit 0200 of its table free. It carries **_B** on the space here, which is what makes
*isprint* and *isgraph* distinguishable - v7's *isprint* answered *false* for a space, which is
a plain violation of C11 sec 7.4.1.8.

### toupper and tolower could not stay macros

v7's are *unconditional* - **(c) - 'a' + 'A'** - so v7's *toupper('1')* returns garbage, where
C11 sec 7.4.2 requires the argument back unchanged when it is not a character of the other
case. The conditional form must test the class and then yield the argument, that is, *evaluate
it twice,* which sec 7.1.4 forbids of a macro.

So both are real functions here, and **v7's unconditional pair keeps the name v7 itself gave it
on other machines: _toupper and _tolower.** A caller that has already checked the class can
still have the cheap one.

*Isblank* went the same way, for want of a bit rather than for the fold: it is the space and
the tab, and the tab's only class is _S, which also covers newline, vertical tab, form feed and
carriage return. A macro would have had to test the two characters separately.

### The table is 129 entries, not 128

Every macro indexes **(_ctype_ + 1)[c]**, so EOF (-1) lands on a leading zero and classifies as
nothing.

**char is unsigned here,** so a byte of 0200..0377 is reachable and runs off the end of the
table. Two places in the tree had to be fixed for that: strtol(3)'s scanner, and a *isspace*()
in libcurses applied to a window cell carrying a standout bit.

### The whole table is 22 words

Six chars to a word.

### The functions are declared but not all defined

C11 sec 7.4 wants each classification to exist as a function as well as a macro, so that
**(isalpha)(c)** and **&isalpha** work. <ctype.h> declares them all; *toupper*, *tolower* and
*isblank* are the three that exist. The rest are a TODO, and the macros serve every caller in
this tree today.
